sableSandbox":true}},{"tool_name":"PowerShell","tool_use_id":"toolu_014pAWE3L1TXXDzMDZLgUbE8","tool_input":{"command":"Set-Location C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\nWrite-Output \"=== Init:N/M vocabulary in Ch5 / Ch13 ===\"\nSelect-String -Path chapter-05*.md,chapter-13*.md -Pattern 'Init:[0-9A-Za-z/]+' -AllMatches | ForEach-Object { \"$($_.Filename):$($_.LineNumber)\" } | Select-Object -First 12\nWrite-Output \"=== Ch8 bootstrap tooling ===\"\nSelect-String -Path chapter-08*.md -Pattern 'minikube|k3s|kubeadm' | Select-Object -First 4 | ForEach-Object { \"$($_.LineNumber): $($_.Line.Substring(0,[Math]::Min(110,$_.Line.Length)))\" }\nWrite-Output \"=== margin-icon density (shipped chapters) ===\"\nforeach ($f in (Get-ChildItem chapter-0[5-9]*.md,chapter-1[0-5]*.md)) {\n  $n = (Select-String -Path $f.FullName -Pattern 'Worth Securing:|Snag:|Closer Look:|Mnemonic:').Count\n  \"{0,-52} {1}\" -f $f.Name, $n\n}","description":"Check Init vocabulary, Ch8 tooling, margin icon density"}},{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_use_id":"toolu_01G9foj3z3gnczeTeMyFtmQd","tool_input":{"command":"for f in chapter-11*.md chapter-12*.md chapter-13*.md; do echo \"--- $f\"; grep -n \"Safe Harbor\" \"$f\" | head -3; grep -n \"^## ☀️\" \"$f\" | head -2; done","description":"Check Safe Harbor vs Zenith ordering convention","dangerouslyDisableSandbox":true}},{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_use_id":"toolu_01LnrfNV64bBXn2U6gjVLEj9","tool_input":{"command":"f=.pipeline-state/ch-16/draft-v2.md; echo \"total cross-bearings: $(grep -o 'cross-bearing: see Ch [0-9]* §[0-9]*' $f | wc -l)\"; echo \"distinct targets:\"; grep -o 'cross-bearing: see Ch [0-9]* §[0-9]*' $f | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn","description":"Count cross-bearings in the Ch16 draft","dangerouslyDisableSandbox":true}},{"tool_name":"Write","tool_use_id":"toolu_01STMBXr8PrcJoJyeoy6toF1","tool_input":{"file_path":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-16\\integration.md","content":"# Integration Check — KCNA Chapter 16\n\n**Checked against:** shipped `chapter-01` … `chapter-15` on disk (all fifteen exist), the B6 section skeleton, and the B7 term ownership ledger.\n**Note on method:** cross-bearings were verified mechanically against the skeleton *and* against the shipped section headings of Ch 9–13, which agree with each other. Facts were not re-audited against snapshots (stage 6 owns that).\n\n## Summary\n\n- Terminology consistency: **pass**\n- Callbacks to earlier chapters: **51 cross-bearings across 24 distinct targets — 51 correct / 0 incorrect.** All 9 inbound pointers from Ch 5, 9, 12, 13 land on the right section. One inbound pointer's *topic gloss* has been invalidated by this revision (see §\"Contradictions\", item 1b).\n- Retrieval-practice accuracy: **fail** — 6 of 7 tagged items correct; Bearings 3 Q2 (`[retrieval: ch11]`) asks for vocabulary Ch 11 never introduces.\n- Glossary coverage: **28 concepts introduced or re-surfaced; 26 defined in the chapter; 6 require glossary entries, 4 of which also require new term-ledger rows.**\n- Contradictions with earlier canon: **1 flagged, and it is a blocker.**\n- Ethical guardrails (skill Part 14): **fail** — 3 exam-frequency claims against the book's own ratified convention.\n\n---\n\n## Terminology consistency\n\nChecked the terms carrying cross-chapter risk. No casing or naming drift found. Lowercase `statefulset` / `endpointslice` occurrences are all inside source tags and CLI resource names (`kubectl get endpointslices`), which is correct.\n\n| Term | Canonical form | Occurrences | Drift? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| init container | `init container`, lowercase two words | ~60 | No — matches Ch 5 §3 and Ch 13 §2 |\n| ephemeral container | `ephemeral container`, lowercase | 39 | No |\n| StatefulSet | CamelCase, unspaced | ~40 | No |\n| EndpointSlice | CamelCase | 12 prose | No |\n| `targetPort` / `port` | code style, exact case | ~32 | No |\n| distroless | lowercase adjective | 11 | No |\n| PersistentVolumeClaim / PVC | CamelCase; PVC after first expansion | ~10 | No |\n| ConfigMap / Secret | CamelCase | ~12 | No |\n| headless Service | lowercase adjective + capital S | 8 | No |\n| ServiceAccount | CamelCase | 3 | No |\n| Pod | capitalized for the object | throughout | No |\n| kubectl / kubelet / etcd | always lowercase | throughout | No |\n| control plane | bare = the cluster's (sense A) | 4 | No |\n| The Voyage Ahead | locked closing-section name | 1 | No |\n| Navigational Hazards / Zenith | post-v5.5 names | 2 / 1 | No — no \"Shoals Ahead\" or \"Landfall\" |\n\n**One borderline, no action needed.** §7 writes \"*their* webhooks, and *their* network policy\" — lowercase, spaced. Read in context it is the generic sense, parallel to the generic \"webhooks\" beside it, not the `NetworkPolicy` object. Canonical forms reserves CamelCase for the object only, so this is compliant. Flagged only so a later sweep doesn't \"correct\" it.\n\n**Ordinal-count convention (ratified 2026-08-30): compliant.** The chapter names patterns without asserting running counts. §8's \"This is a shape you have met before in this book\" is exactly the sanctioned form.\n\n**House heading and marker forms: compliant.** `## <difficulty> §N — Title` (the Ch 5–8 form the skeleton recommends), `☀️` on the closing section, one Zenith.\n\n---\n\n## Callback correctness\n\nEvery `Ch N §M` pointer was checked against the skeleton and, for Ch 9–13, against the shipped headings. All 24 distinct targets resolve:\n\nCh 2 §2 · Ch 4 §3 · Ch 4 §4 · Ch 5 §3 · Ch 5 §5 · Ch 5 §7 · Ch 6 §6 · Ch 8 §1 · Ch 8 §2 · Ch 8 §5 · Ch 9 §3 · Ch 9 §4 · Ch 9 §5 · Ch 9 §6 · Ch 9 §7 · Ch 10 §2 · Ch 11 §6 · Ch 12 §2 · Ch 12 §6 · Ch 13 §1 · Ch 13 §2 · Ch 13 §3 · Ch 13 §4 · Ch 13 §5.\n\nProse callbacks verified against the shipped text rather than assumed:\n\n| Claim in Ch 16 | Verified |\n|---|---|\n| \"Chapter 13 taught you to stop reaching for the logs and read the phase first\" | ✓ Ch 13's subtitle is *\"Read the phase before you read the logs\"*; its §8 is *\"Read the Phase First\"* |\n| §1's restatement of the mechanical scope test | ✓ Matches `chapter-13:384` almost word for word |\n| \"Chapter 8's kubectl verb table pointed here explicitly for `exec`\" | ✓ `chapter-08:364` — `` | `exec` | Execute a command against a container in a Pod | ahead, in Ch 16 | `` |\n| \"Chapter 12 pointed here for the debug-container case\" | ✓ `chapter-12:1342`, and 1342 falls inside §6 (1212–1443), so the `Ch 12 §6` attribution is exact |\n| §3's reconciliation of the two inbound phrasings | ✓ Ch 13 says \"getting inside a container\"; Ch 12 says \"getting inside, and adding what isn't there\". Both exist; the draft names both and tells the reader they are the same section. Good catch by the revision. |\n\n**Reciprocal pairs intact.** Ch 13 §1 ↔ Ch 16 §1, Ch 13 §2 ↔ Ch 16 §2, Ch 13 §5 ↔ Ch 16 §3, Ch 9 §4 ↔ Ch 16 §4, Ch 12 §6 ↔ Ch 16 §3 all point both ways at the right numbers.\n\n---\n\n## Retrieval-practice accuracy\n\n| Item | Tag | Topic | Covered there? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bearings 1 Q6 | `ch2` | An image contains only what was put in it | ✓ Ch 2 §2 |\n| Bearings 2 Q2 | `ch9` | `port` vs `targetPort` | ✓ Ch 9 §3 |\n| Bearings 2 Q4 | `ch5` | liveness vs readiness independence | ✓ Ch 5 §7 |\n| Bearings 3 Q2 | `ch11` | default `persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy` for `whenDeleted` **and** `whenScaled` | **✗ partial — see below** |\n| Practice Q12 | `ch9` | short-name DNS resolves in the client's namespace | ✓ Ch 9 §7 |\n| Practice Q13 | `ch9` | `targetPort` must match the container's listening port | ✓ Ch 9 §3 (see note) |\n| Practice Q14 | `ch11` | a StatefulSet PVC survives Pod deletion | ✓ Ch 11 §6 — `chapter-11:1235`, `:1412` |\n\n**Bearings 3 Q2 is misaligned.** Ch 11 does teach the fact — `chapter-11:1235` quotes *\"Retain (default): PVCs from the volumeClaimTemplate are not affected when their Pod is deleted\"* and *\"the default for policies is Retain.\"* But Ch 11 never names the field `persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy`, and the strings `whenDeleted` and `whenScaled` appear **nowhere in the shipped chapter**. The stem asks for the default \"for both the `whenDeleted` and `whenScaled` cases,\" and distractor C (\"Retain for `whenDeleted`, Delete for `whenScaled`\") cannot be evaluated without the two-case split. A reader can only answer from §6's Closer Look — which makes this an in-chapter item wearing a retrieval tag, and it silently penalises anyone who trusts the tag and reaches back to Ch 11.\n\nTwo clean repairs, author's choice:\n- **Reword to the fact Ch 11 taught:** \"You delete a StatefulSet replica's Pod. What happens to its PVC, and is that the default or a configured choice?\" Keeps the tag honest, keeps the Q1 dependency.\n- **Or drop the tag** and let it stand as a §6 item. The checkpoint's preamble (\"One tests material from an earlier chapter\") then needs adjusting, and Practice Q14 already carries the Ch 11 retrieval load.\n\n**Practice Q13, note only, no fix needed.** The tag is defensible — the graded knowledge is `targetPort` = the container's port, which is Ch 9 §3's. That its *scenario* is §5's port-forward trap makes it an interleaving item, which is the intent.\n\n**Density.** 7 tagged items in 33 graded questions ≈ 21%, inside the 20–25% band. Per checkpoint: Bearings 1 1/6, Bearings 2 2/5, Bearings 3 1/5, Practice 3/17. Each checkpoint's stated count matches its contents.\n\n---\n\n## Glossary coverage\n\n| Concept/command introduced | Defined in-chapter? | Needs glossary entry? |\n|---|---|---|\n| distroless image | yes | **yes** — and see the correction below |\n| ephemeral container | yes | yes |\n| `kubectl debug` (three shapes) | yes | yes |\n| `--copy-to` | yes | no — covered by the `kubectl debug` entry |\n| `--target` | partially (principle only; flag behavior deferred) | no |\n| debug profile / `--profile` | shape only, deliberately | no — taught as a shape, ungraded |\n| `kubectl debug node/` | yes | no — covered by `kubectl debug` |\n| `kubectl exec` | yes | yes |\n| `kubectl port-forward` | yes | yes |\n| `pods/portforward` subresource | glossed in context | no |\n| `Init:N/M`, `Init:Error`, `Init:CrashLoopBackOff` | yes, §2 | **yes — and no ledger row exists** |\n| termination message / `/dev/termination-log` | yes, §2 | **yes — and no ledger row exists** |\n| `persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy`, `whenDeleted`, `whenScaled` | yes, §6 Closer Look | **yes — and no ledger row exists** |\n| Telepresence | yes, §7 | yes (low priority; third-party proper noun) |\n| silently-dropped manifest field | yes, §3 | no — a concept tag, not a term |\n| the four triage questions | yes, §1 | no — ledger row exists, book-internal device |\n| ordering deadlock / non-idempotency | yes, §2 | no |\n| selector/label mismatch; port-vs-targetPort; headless-Service peer DNS; PVC survival; PSA `restricted`; probes; QoS; scope boundary | referred with pointers, correctly not redefined | no |\n\n**Correction to an AUTHOR-REVIEW comment in the draft.** The comment at §3 states that \"distroless\" *\"has no owner in the term ledger and no ambient-tier row.\"* That is wrong twice over. The B7 ledger's Ch 16 block carries the row **\"Distroless image (and the debugging problem it creates) | Ch 16 §3 | first appears Ch 2 | name only, always with a pointer.\"** This chapter is the term's **owner**, and — since `distroless` appears in no shipped chapter, Ch 2 included — this is also its **first appearance in the book**. Both facts are the opposite of what the comment says. Delete or rewrite the comment before it sends stage 14 hunting for a non-problem. The glossary entry is still wanted; ownership is simply already settled.\n\nTwo ledger rows are now stale and should be corrected at the next B7 pass (neither affects this chapter's text):\n- \"Distroless image … first appears Ch 2\" — Ch 2 never names it.\n- \"Empty EndpointSlice (as a symptom) | Ch 16 §4\" — this revision deliberately splits *empty* (selector) from *present-but-not-ready* (readiness). The row should read \"no ready endpoints,\" assuming finding 1 resolves in this chapter's favour.\n\n**`Init:N/M` is the one that matters.** It appears in no shipped chapter — Ch 13 §2 handles init failures through the container-state reason `PodInitializing`, never the STATUS-column string. Ch 16 introduces the vocabulary and then grades it hard: Bearings 1 Q2 (`Init:1/3`) and Practice Q3 (`Init:2/4`) both turn on reading it. It is defined in §2, so the reader is not stranded — but per the ledger's own rule that a term in graded text may not be glossary-only, it needs a row plus a glossary entry, exactly as Ch 13's gate queued `crictl` and `ProgressDeadlineExceeded`.\n\n---\n\n## Contradictions with earlier canon\n\n### 1. **BLOCKER — Ch 16 §4 and shipped Ch 9 §4 teach opposite EndpointSlice membership rules, and both are graded.**\n\nThis is the one thing on this page that must not reach print unresolved.\n\n**Shipped Ch 9 §4 teaches that a not-ready Pod is *not in the slice*:**\n- `chapter-09:740` ★ Fixed Point — *\"A Pod must both **match the selector** and be **Ready** to appear in the Service's EndpointSlice and receive traffic.\"*\n- `chapter-09:844` Bearings #2 item 2 — selector matches four Pods, one failing readiness; the published answer at `:868` is **three**, and the why-wrong at `:874` explicitly refutes \"Four\": *\"matching the labels makes a Pod eligible for the endpoint list; it does not put the Pod on it.\"*\n- `chapter-09:868`, `:1602` — *\"the endpoints controller removes the Pod's IP address from the endpoints of all Services that match the Pod\"* [source: k8s-docs-pod-lifecycle-2026-08-23]\n- `chapter-09:1418` Practice Q11 and `:1729` summary row carry the same rule.\n\n**Ch 16 §4 teaches the opposite:**\n- *\"**All** the matching Pods — readiness is not a filter on membership.\"*\n- *\"a selector that matches nothing leaves the slice with no endpoints at all. Readiness that is failing leaves the endpoints exactly where they are, marked not ready.\"*\n- Bearings 2 Q1 grades it: three endpoints, all `ready: false`, and answer A is refuted on the grounds that a selector miss *\"would leave the slice with **no endpoints at all**, not three unready ones.\"*\n- The Exam Alert trap table names the Ch 9 position as the error to unlearn: *\"**Believing a not-ready Pod is removed from its EndpointSlice** — It is included and marked not ready.\"*\n\nA reader who works Ch 9's checkpoint and then Ch 16's gets marked wrong once in each direction by the same book. Ch 16 additionally tells them the thing Ch 9 taught them is a trap.\n\n**This is not a case of one chapter being carelessly wrong.** Both cite snapshots, and the upstream documentation is itself inconsistent across pages: the pod-lifecycle page Ch 9 quotes retains the older \"removes the IP address\" phrasing, while the EndpointSlice page Ch 16 quotes describes membership plus a `ready` condition. Adjudicating which is current is stage 6's call, not mine.\n\nWhat I can add is that **Ch 9 already contains the machinery to state the general rule correctly**, which makes the repair small if the ruling goes Ch 16's way:\n- `chapter-09:750` already says terminating Pods are *\"not immediately removed from EndpointSlices\"* and *\"always have their `ready` status as `false`.\"*\n- `chapter-09:754`'s Closer Look already introduces the `serving` condition.\n\nSo Ch 9 teaches the include-and-mark model for the *terminating* case and the remove model for the *readiness* case. Generalising the former is a contained edit — the Fixed Point at `:740`, the Bearings answer at `:868`/`:874` (count changes 3 → 4 with one endpoint not ready), Practice Q11's answer at `:1602`, and the two table rows at `:1312`/`:1729`.\n\n**Author decision required.** Whichever way it goes, the losing chapter's graded text changes, and both chapters must move in the same commit. Ch 16 should not ship its Exam Alert row calling the other chapter's teaching a trap until this is settled.\n\n### 1b. Downstream symptom of the same issue — Ch 9's inbound pointer gloss\n\n`chapter-09:766` reads: *\"Chapters 13 and 16 own the troubleshooting workflow, and they will retrieve this by name. [cross-bearing: see Ch 16 §4 — **a Service whose endpoint list is empty**]\"*\n\nThe section number resolves correctly. The topic gloss no longer describes what §4 teaches — §4's whole point is now that the list is often *not* empty. Fix in the same commit as finding 1; if Ch 16's model stands, retarget to \"a Service with no ready endpoints.\"\n\n### 2. Checked and clear — no other conflicts\n\n- **PSA refusing a debug container.** The draft's AUTHOR-REVIEW worries this rests only on Ch 12. It does, and **Ch 12 shipped it**: `chapter-12:1342` — *\"And a debug container is a container. In a namespace enforcing `restricted`, a debugging tool that tries to inject a container with elevated privileges can be refused by admission … [cross-bearing: see Ch 16 §3 — getting inside, and adding what isn't there].\"* Consistent, reciprocal, and Practice Q9's correct answer is backed by shipped canon. The snapshot gap remains stage 6's item; the integration status is clean.\n- **Init-container diagnosis.** Ch 13 §2 reads `PodInitializing` via `describe`; Ch 16 §2 reads `Init:N/M` via `get`. Different surfaces, complementary, and §2 names `describe` as the place the init containers are listed. No conflict.\n- **Scope boundary and triage order.** §1 matches `chapter-13:382–390` exactly, including the one-way-handoff framing.\n- **`port`/`targetPort`, probes, PVC survival, headless Service, DNS search domains.** All glossed-and-pointed rather than redefined, consistent with their owners.\n\n---\n\n## Ethical guardrails check\n\n- [x] No fabricated statistics or claims — the revision correctly cut the unsupported \"the domain doubled\" claim, and the authored `~4%` allocation is labelled as authored beside the sourced 16%\n- [x] Fear-based content uses real examples — none present; the closest is \"engineers have spent days on it from that angle,\" which is practitioner-oriented\n- [x] Simplification acknowledged — Dead Reckoning in Why This Chapter Matters; the debug-profile source conflict is handled with \"include\" rather than a closed list; the port-forward figure honestly stops at the API server\n- [x] Authority claims cite legitimate sources\n- [ ] **\"Frequently tested\" claims are verifiable against the curriculum snapshot — FAIL**\n- [x] No strawmanning of alternative study methods\n- [x] Subject dignity — humour is aimed at practitioners throughout, and §1 explicitly disarms the platform-team-as-adversary reading\n\n**The failing item.** The book has a ratified convention against exam-frequency claims, stated in four places: `chapter-12:1873` (*\"This book will tell you when material is high-yield by reasoning about the published objectives, and it will not manufacture a statistic to make the point land harder\"*), `chapter-14:34–36`, `chapter-15:411` (*\"nothing here is described as 'frequently tested' or 'commonly appears' … What you will see instead is 'easy to confuse'\"*), and `chapter-lineup.md:184`. KCNA publishes no sub-topic list, so there is no basis for a frequency claim.\n\nThree lines in this chapter cross it:\n\n| Location | Text | Suggested |\n|---|---|---|\n| Why This Chapter Matters | \"What gets tested here is not flag syntax. It is which verb answers which question\" | \"What this competency rewards is not flag syntax…\" |\n| §3 ★ Fixed Point | \"and it is the fact the exam reaches for\" | \"and it is the fact the rest of this section rests on\" |\n| Exam Alert #1 | \"This is the shape the exam favors for this competency\" | \"This is the distinction the material rewards\" |\n\nFor contrast, §3's \"the constraints are exam material\" is fine — `chapter-11:559` uses the same construction, and it is a yield claim rather than a frequency claim.\n\n---\n\n## Recommended fixes\n\nOrdered. Only items the diagnostics and the revision did not already resolve.\n\n1. **Resolve the EndpointSlice readiness-membership contradiction with Ch 9 §4 (finding 1).** Blocker. Requires an author ruling plus a coordinated edit to both chapters' graded text, and a fix to Ch 9's inbound pointer gloss at `chapter-09:766` (finding 1b) in the same commit.\n\n2. **Fix Bearings 3 Q2's retrieval tag.** Either reword the stem to the fact Ch 11 actually shipped, or drop `[retrieval: ch11]` and adjust the checkpoint preamble.\n\n3. **Soften the three exam-frequency claims** to the convention Ch 12, 14 and 15 already follow.\n\n4. **Delete or rewrite the \"distroless\" AUTHOR-REVIEW comment.** It asserts the opposite of what the B7 ledger says; left in place it will send stage 14 looking for an owner that already exists — this chapter.\n\n5. **Queue for the glossary build, with new term-ledger rows for the first three:** `Init:N/M` / `Init:Error` / `Init:CrashLoopBackOff` (graded, highest priority), termination message + `/dev/termination-log`, `persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy` / `whenDeleted` / `whenScaled`, distroless image, ephemeral container, `kubectl debug` / `exec` / `port-forward`, Telepresence.\n\n6. **Move 🏆 Safe Harbor to after the ☀️ §8 Zenith.** Every shipped comparable puts it last — Ch 10 (`:1338` Zenith, `:1401` Safe Harbor), Ch 11, Ch 12 (`:1737` / `:2231`), Ch 13 (`:1469` / `:1814`). Ch 16 currently places it before §8, at the end of Bearings 3. The placement is self-aware (\"the practical material is behind you\"), but it diverges from a house pattern that is otherwise unanimous, and it does so in the chapter a reader meets immediately after Ch 13. Author's call; low cost either way.\n\n7. **Rename the Zenith figure anchor** `ch16-zenith-mine-or-the-platforms` → `ch16-fig06-mine-or-the-platforms`, or accept the exception deliberately. The draft already flags this; confirming it as a real join-key risk against `image-specs.md`. If renamed, draft and image-specs must change together.\n\n### Carried forward to stage 14 (frontmatter) — not a defect\n\nThe draft's opening comment asks which stage materialises frontmatter. **Answer: not this one.** `.pipeline-state/ch-13/draft-v2.md` also opens straight at its H1, while shipped `chapter-13` carries full frontmatter. Ch 16 is at exactly the point Ch 13 was; the revision was right not to add one.\n\nThe substantive half of that question does stand, and it is an integration matter:\n\n- Ch 13's frontmatter tags every section `objectives: [\"D2.3\"]`, and its own note at `chapter-13:21` says the twin — *\"D3.2 Debugging, application scope\"* — **is Ch 16**. So Ch 16 must carry `D3.2`.\n- Whether it must **also** carry `D2.3` is the open question the curriculum audit raised: if a slice of D2.3 was deferred here from Ch 13, and neither chapter's frontmatter claims it, that slice ends the book unowned. Ch 13 claims all of D2.3 for itself in frontmatter while deferring application-scope work in prose, so the two do not obviously conflict — but this needs the author's ruling before stage 14 writes the block.\n- Whichever way it goes, `kb_tags.concepts` must gain `termination-message` and `silently-dropped-manifest-field`, both introduced by this revision.\n\n### Confirmed clean — no action\n\nVerified so a later stage does not re-open them: all 51 cross-bearings and all 9 inbound pointers resolve; the ordinal-count convention is honoured; margin-icon density (16) sits inside the shipped range of 5–17 and matches Ch 11 exactly, so the skill's nominal 3–8 target is a book-wide divergence rather than a Ch 16 finding; heading form, Zenith marking, and the locked \"The Voyage Ahead\" name are all correct; Soundings runs 8 questions, is answerable from prerequisites, and spoils none of the chapter's four Fixed Points.\n"}}],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":31,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"I couldn't write `.pipeline-state/ch-16/integration.md` — the write permission wasn't granted in this session. The report is complete and ready to write on approval. Here are the findings.\n\n## Summary\n\n- Terminology consistency: **pass**\n- Callbacks: **51 cross-bearings / 24 distinct targets — all correct.** All 9 inbound pointers from Ch 5, 9, 12, 13 resolve.\n- Retrieval-practice accuracy: **fail** (6 of 7 correct)\n- Glossary coverage: 28 concepts, 26 defined in-chapter, **6 need entries, 4 need new ledger rows**\n- Contradictions with earlier canon: **1, and it's a blocker**\n- Ethical guardrails: **fail** — 3 exam-frequency claims\n\n## The blocker: Ch 16 §4 contradicts shipped Ch 9 §4, and both are graded\n\nShipped Ch 9 teaches that a not-ready Pod is **not in the EndpointSlice**:\n- `chapter-09:740` Fixed Point — \"A Pod must both **match the selector** and be **Ready** to appear in the Service's EndpointSlice\"\n- `chapter-09:844` Bearings #2: four matching Pods, one failing readiness → published answer is **three**, and `:874` explicitly refutes \"Four\"\n- `chapter-09:868`, `:1602`, `:1729` carry the same rule\n\nCh 16 §4 teaches the opposite (\"**All** the matching Pods — readiness is not a filter on membership\"), grades it at Bearings 2 Q1, and its Exam Alert names the Ch 9 position as a trap to unlearn. A reader gets marked wrong once in each direction by the same book.\n\nBoth chapters cite snapshots; the upstream docs are themselves inconsistent across pages. Adjudicating that is stage 6's call. What's useful here: **Ch 9 already contains the machinery to state it Ch 16's way** — `:750` says terminating Pods are \"not immediately removed from EndpointSlices\" with `ready: false`, and `:754` already introduces the `serving` condition. So if the ruling favours Ch 16, the Ch 9 repair is contained: the Fixed Point, one Bearings answer (3→4), Practice Q11's answer, two table rows. Needs an author ruling; both chapters move in one commit.\n\nSame commit should fix `chapter-09:766`, whose inbound gloss \"*a Service whose endpoint list is empty*\" no longer describes a section built on the list often not being empty.\n\n## Other findings\n\n**Retrieval fail — Bearings 3 Q2** (`[retrieval: ch11]`): Ch 11 teaches the Retain default (`:1235`, `:1412`) but never names `persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy`, and `whenDeleted`/`whenScaled` appear nowhere in the shipped chapter. Distractor C can't be evaluated without that vocabulary. Reword to the fact Ch 11 shipped, or drop the tag.\n\n**Guardrails** — the book has a ratified no-frequency-claims convention (`chapter-12:1873`, `chapter-14:34`, `chapter-15:411`, `chapter-lineup:184`). Three lines cross it: \"What gets tested here is not flag syntax\", \"the fact the exam reaches for\", \"the shape the exam favors\". §3's \"the constraints are exam material\" is fine — `chapter-11:559` uses the same construction.\n\n**The draft's own \"distroless\" AUTHOR-REVIEW is wrong.** It says the term has no ledger owner; B7 assigns it to **Ch 16 §3**, and since it appears in no shipped chapter, this is also its first appearance in the book. Delete the comment before stage 14 chases it.\n\n**Glossary/ledger:** `Init:N/M` family is the priority — it appears in no shipped chapter (Ch 13 uses `PodInitializing`), yet Bearings 1 Q2 and Practice Q3 both grade it. Also termination message, the PVC retention field names, distroless, Telepresence.\n\n**Safe Harbor placement** — Ch 16 puts 🏆 before the §8 Zenith; Ch 10, 11, 12, 13 all put it after, unanimously. Author's call.\n\n## Answering the draft's open questions\n\n- **Frontmatter is not this stage's gap.** `ch-13/draft-v2.md` also has none; shipped Ch 13 does. T